---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = 'https://www.dg-sdk.com';
const url = base + '/blog/static-voltage-capacitance';
const title = 'Static Voltage and Capacitance: Same Charge, Different Voltage';
const seoTitle = 'Static Voltage and Capacitance: Q/C Explained';
const description = 'See how capacitance changes voltage at fixed charge through an ideal capacitor example, and why the result cannot be used as a direct film-charge calculation.';
---

<Layout title={seoTitle + ' | Shidike'} description={description} canonical={url}
  image="/assets/img/blog/capacitance-static-voltage-v1.jpg">
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: base },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: base + '/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: title, item: url }
    ]
  })} />
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BlogPosting', headline: title, description,
    image: base + '/assets/img/blog/capacitance-static-voltage-v1.jpg',
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', url: base },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'Shidike', legalName: 'Dongguan Shidike Intelligent Equipment Co., Ltd.', url: base },
    datePublished: '2026-11-15', dateModified: '2026-11-15', mainEntityOfPage: url
  })} />

  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-head">
      <div class="container post-col">
        <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>Static Voltage and Capacitance</div>
        <div class="post-tags"><span class="blog-tag">Static Control</span><span class="blog-tag">Capacitance</span><span class="blog-tag">Charge and Voltage</span></div>
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <p class="post-lead">A higher voltage does not always mean a larger amount of charge. An ideal capacitor gives a useful counterexample: its voltage can rise while the charge on each plate stays fixed. The key is to identify what changed and what the model holds constant.</p>
        <div class="post-info"><span>November 15, 2026</span><span>Shidike Technical Team</span><span>3 min read</span></div>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="container post-col post-body">
      <picture class="post-figure">
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/capacitance-static-voltage-v1-mobile.webp" type="image/webp" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source media="(max-width: 600px)" srcset="/assets/img/blog/capacitance-static-voltage-v1-mobile.jpg" type="image/jpeg" width="800" height="1400" />
        <source srcset="/assets/img/blog/capacitance-static-voltage-v1.webp" type="image/webp" />
        <img src="/assets/img/blog/capacitance-static-voltage-v1.jpg" alt="Two ideal capacitor cases with fixed plate-charge magnitude of 2 nC: 20 pF gives 100 V and 10 pF gives 200 V. Values are hypothetical." width="1600" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </picture>
      <p><em>Original concept diagram with hypothetical values. It represents two states of an ideal capacitor, not equipment measurements or instructions for a physical test.</em></p>

      <section id="charge-and-voltage">
        <h2>Charge and voltage describe different quantities</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://www.esda.org/esd-overview/esd-fundamentals/part-1-an-introduction-to-esd/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EOS/ESD Association's introduction to ESD</a> distinguishes charge, measured in coulombs, from electrostatic potential, expressed in volts, and relates them through capacitance. A voltage value alone does not specify an amount of charge.</p>
        <p>For a defined capacitor, the relationship can be written:</p>
        <p class="formula">Q = C × V &nbsp; → &nbsp; V = Q / C</p>
        <p>Here <strong>C</strong> is capacitance in farads. Following <a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/8-1-capacitors-and-capacitance" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenStax's capacitor definition</a>, <strong>Q</strong> is the charge magnitude on either plate, with opposite signs on the two plates; <strong>V</strong> is the potential difference between them. Q is not the sum of the two signed plate charges.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="fixed-charge-example">
        <h2>Work through a fixed-charge example</h2>
        <p>Choose Q = 2 nC for both states. Let the capacitance be 20 pF in case A and 10 pF in case B. These inputs are invented for the calculation; neither case describes a Shidike product or a customer's process.</p>
        <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Hypothetical fixed-charge capacitance comparison">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Quantity</th><th scope="col">Case A</th><th scope="col">Case B</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><th scope="row">Plate-charge magnitude Q</th><td>2 nC</td><td>2 nC</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Capacitance C</th><td>20 pF</td><td>10 pF</td></tr>
              <tr><th scope="row">Calculated V = Q / C</th><td>100 V</td><td>200 V</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>For case A, (2 × 10<sup>−9</sup>) / (20 × 10<sup>−12</sup>) = 100 V. For case B, the same numerator divided by 10 × 10<sup>−12</sup> gives 200 V. The voltage doubles because the assumed capacitance halves; no extra plate charge was added in this model.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="geometry-and-connections">
        <h2>State the geometry and electrical connection</h2>
        <p>The <a href="https://labs.phys.utk.edu/mbreinig/phys222core/modules/m2/capacitors.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Tennessee's capacitor lesson</a> explains how geometry and the material between the plates determine capacitance. For the ideal parallel-plate approximation, C = εA/d. With plate area A and permittivity ε unchanged, doubling separation d halves C. Edge effects are neglected; the separation must remain small relative to the plate dimensions.</p>
        <p>The diagram also assumes no charge exchange: the voltage source is disconnected and leakage is excluded. If an ideal source instead maintains a fixed voltage, the same change in capacitance changes Q. Constant charge and constant voltage are different conditions.</p>
        <p>As a separate hypothetical calculation, a source holding 100 V would give Q = 2 nC at 20 pF and Q = 1 nC at 10 pF. Label which condition applies before comparing the two cases.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="application-boundary">
        <h2>Use the model to ask a better application question</h2>
        <p>For a static-control discussion, separate three statements: the reported voltage changed; the capacitance changed; the charge changed. The first does not establish the other two. The calculation above is a counterexample, not a diagnosis of a production line.</p>
        <p>The EOS/ESD Association also notes that insulating materials can hold charge at different locations. An unspecified film and roller arrangement is not automatically the two-conductor capacitor used here. Do not enter a local film reading and an assumed capacitance into this example to claim the film's total charge.</p>
        <p>For instrument terminology, see <a href="/blog/surface-resistance-vs-surface-voltage">surface resistance versus surface voltage</a>. For selecting a control method, start with <a href="/guides/how-to-prevent-static">the static-prevention guide</a>. This model does not calculate ionizer balance, decay time or a machine setting.</p>
      </section>
      <div class="post-cta">
        <h2>Discuss the actual static-control application</h2>
        <p>Share the material, the affected operation and the measurement report with Shidike. Include what the instrument reports and where the observation was made so the application review starts with the actual setup.</p>
        <p><a class="send-cta" href="/products/static-eliminator">Explore Static Eliminators &rarr;</a><a class="send-alt" href="/contact?type=selection&amp;product=Static%20Voltage%20Application%20Review">Discuss your static-control application &rarr;</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .post-col { max-width: 780px; }
    .post-head { background: #f7f8fa; padding: 48px 0 34px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .post-head .breadcrumb { font-size: 13px; color: #7d8d97; margin-bottom: 18px; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb a { color: var(--brand-deep); text-decoration: none; }
    .post-head .breadcrumb span { margin: 0 8px; }
    .post-tags { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; margin-bottom: 14px; }
    .blog-tag {
      font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em; text-transform: uppercase;
      background: #eef4f8; color: #4a6373; padding: 4px 9px; border-radius: 999px;
    }
    .post-head h1 {
      font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 2.5rem); line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 800;
      letter-spacing: -.02em; color: var(--navy); margin: 0 0 14px;
    }
    .post-lead { font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.75; color: #46545f; margin: 0 0 18px; }
    .post-info { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 16px; font-size: 12.5px; color: #8a99a4; }

    .post-body { padding: 34px 0 70px; }
    .post-figure { display: block; margin: 0 0 30px; }
    .post-figure img {
      display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px;
    }
    .post-body h2 {
      font-size: 1.42rem; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: -.01em; color: var(--navy);
      margin: 34px 0 12px;
    }
    .post-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 15px; }
    .post-body a { color: var(--brand-deep); }
    .post-body h3 {
      font-size: 1.12rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); margin: 26px 0 10px;
    }
    .post-body ul, .post-body ol { margin: 0 0 15px; padding-left: 22px; }
    .post-body li { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.78; color: #3d4a54; margin: 0 0 7px; }
    .post-body blockquote, .post-body .blog-quote {
      margin: 0 0 18px; padding: 13px 16px; background: #f2f6f9;
      border-left: 3px solid var(--brand-deep); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    }
    .post-body .blog-quote p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    .post-body .step-block { margin: 0 0 22px; }
    .post-body .step-num {
      display: inline-block; font-size: 11px; font-weight: 700; letter-spacing: .06em;
      text-transform: uppercase; background: var(--navy); color: #fff;
      padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 999px; margin-bottom: 8px;
    }
    .formula {
      font-size: 1.2rem !important; font-weight: 700; text-align: center; color: var(--navy) !important;
      background: #f2f6f9; border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px;
    }

    .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 20px; }
    .post-body table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14.5px; }
    .post-body th, .post-body td {
      text-align: left; padding: 10px 12px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); vertical-align: top;
    }
    .post-body thead th { background: #f2f6f9; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); }
    .post-body tbody th { width: 22%; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); background: #fafbfc; }

    .faq-list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; margin: 6px 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item { padding: 15px 4px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline); }
    .faq-item summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 700; color: var(--navy); font-size: 15.5px; }
    .faq-item p { margin: 10px 0 0; }

    .post-cta {
      margin-top: 40px; padding: 26px 26px 22px; background: #f7f8fa;
      border: 1px solid var(--hairline); border-radius: 10px;
    }
    .post-cta h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.25rem; }
    .send-cta, .send-alt { display: inline-block; font-weight: 700; margin-right: 22px; }
    @media (max-width: 767px) {
      .post-body { padding-left: 16px; padding-right: 16px; }
    }
  </style>
</Layout>
